 09_8	Behind each bar was a single division. Count the handfuls of five from the first sixty boxes, and divide by all the handfuls of five from the whole company. At a hundred and seven boxes, that's just over five percent.
 09_9	If the friends had ten boxes instead, with the same largest number, would the range get wider or narrower?
 09_10	Narrower. Ten numbers all sixty or less would be rare from a big company, so the range shrinks to sixty through seventy-nine.
+10_1	Here's another recipe, built on a different idea. A company's numbers run from one up to the count, so they balance at the middle number. With seventy-one boxes the middle is thirty-six, and the count is twice the middle, minus one.
+10_2	A fair handful's numbers should spread around the middle too, so let the handful's average stand in for it. Twice the average, minus one. Call it the doubling recipe.
+10_3	Our five add up to a hundred and eighty-five, an average of thirty-seven. So doubling says seventy-three, just above the gap recipe's seventy-one.
+10_4	It's unbiased too. In a fair handful each box is just as likely to carry any number from one to the count, so averaged over every handful, the handful's average is the middle number. That makes the doubling recipe average the count, whatever the count is.
+10_5	So which recipe should we trust? Try a strange handful. Keep the box at sixty, and make the other four two, three, four and five. What does doubling say for this handful?
+10_6	Twenty-eight point six. That's fewer than the sixty boxes we know were made. The gap recipe says seventy-one for this handful, as it does for every handful topped by sixty.
+10_7	That's no freak. In a company of seventy-one boxes, about one handful in five gets a doubling estimate below its own largest number. The gap recipe never does.
+10_8	So put both recipes side by side, over every handful from a company of exactly seventy-one boxes. The gap recipe's sixty-seven bars come back. The doubling recipe looks only at the total of the five numbers, so its handfuls can be counted by total, and its estimates spread from five all the way to a hundred and thirty-seven.
+10_9	Both balance at seventy-one. But the purple bars are lower and wider. Ignoring direction, doubling misses by fourteen point three boxes on average, the gap recipe by nine point two. The standard deviation, built from squared misses, says seventeen point eight against eleven point seven. By either measure, the gap recipe's estimates spread less.
+10_10	So the gap recipe, which uses only the largest number, beats the recipe that uses all five. What are the other four worth, then?
+11_1	Back to the friends' own five boxes. Suppose all you were told is that the largest number is sixty. Then the other four could be any four different numbers below sixty.
+11_2	Try it on pairs. In the tiny company of five boxes, four of its ten pairs have five as their larger number, and the smaller one is one, two, three or four. A fair draw makes those four pairs equally likely, so each is one time in four.
+11_3	Now let the company make six boxes, or eight. There are more pairs, fifteen and then twenty-eight, so a larger number of five gets rarer. But the pairs topped by five are still the same four, and each is still one time in four.
+11_4	It's the same with sixty. Whatever the count, from sixty boxes up, a handful topped by sixty is sixty plus any four numbers below it. That's over four hundred and fifty thousand groups of four, all equally likely, for every count. Only the chance that sixty is the largest changes. With exactly sixty boxes it's one in twelve, with seventy-one about one in twenty-nine, and with a thousand about one in eighteen million.
+11_5	So once you know the largest number, how much can the other four tell you about the count?
+11_6	Nothing. Given the largest, their pattern is the same for every count, so it can't favour any count over another. Statisticians say the largest number is sufficient. It holds everything a fair handful can tell you about the count.
+11_7	That's why the gap recipe needs only the largest number. Whatever the other four are, the gaps below sixty hold fifty-five numbers, sixty minus five. Ours split them fairly evenly, and the strange handful's put nearly all of them in a single gap.
+11_8	All of this needs the handful to be fair. If bigger numbers were likelier to be picked, the other four could shift with the count and carry a clue of their own. With a fair handful they say nothing more about the count, but they can still warn you when a draw doesn't look fair.
